{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’d like to thank Henson Shaving for supporting\nPBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3600,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You’ve probably heard about the James Webb\nSpace Telescope and seen some cool pictures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7740,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But why should astronomers have all the fun?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10020,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How do we get to use this new toy ourselves?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19200,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The James Webb Space Telescope has now been\nin full operational science mode for several"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weeks now, and has seen some pretty amazing\nstuff, from colliding galaxies to planets"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29640,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and exoplanet atmospheres to the earliest,\nmost distant galaxies ever observed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34740,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can check out its amazing accomplishments\nin lots of places - like the official NASA"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39540,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Goddard Flight Center websites, and all\nover YouTube - but special shoutout to Anton"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44640,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Petrov for the best “best of JWST” that\nI’ve seen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48120,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many of you asked whether we were going to\ndo a JWST episode, and at first I thought"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55200,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it wasn’t needed due to the massive coverage."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58080,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But then I realized there’s something missing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60600,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rather than tell you about what the telescope\nhas done, or will do, I want to talk about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66300,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the process of actually using a telescope\nlike this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69660,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What it takes to get time on the JWST, and\nhow you can use it yourself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74820,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fact is, anyone CAN use JWST."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78540,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was built by NASA with \nsignificant contributions "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81660,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the Canadian and European Space Agencies,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84000,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it was built for everyone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86040,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You don’t have to be employed by NASA, or\na US citizen, or even a professional astronomer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91440,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But before we get to using JWST, let’s talk\nabout the telescope itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95940,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, if you’re going to use it you\nbetter know what it’s useful for."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99360,
    "dDurationMs": 8520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The James Webb Space Telescope, JWST, or “the\nWebb” has been in the works for decades."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107880,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Really, from right after the launch of the\nHubble Space Telescope in 1990."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112320,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The planning picked up over the 90s, and pretty\nquickly core properties started to crystalize"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119520,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for what was then the “Next Generation Space\nTelescope” (NGST)."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124620,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was to be a much bigger scope that Hubble\n- because with telescopes size matters."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130260,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The size of the aperture determines how sensitive\nit is to very faint objects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135240,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hubble has a 2.4 meter diameter mirror - the\nNGST had to be much bigger - 8m diameter to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142920,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make it a worthy upgrade, which would give\nit 6 times the collecting area of Hubble."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147840,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To get an 8m mirror into \nspace under the incredible "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151260,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stresses of space launch, it was realized"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154320,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "early on that a foldable mirror was the way\nto go."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158040,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another early decision that persisted was\nthe focus on the infrared light - and I’ll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162780,
    "dDurationMs": 660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "come back to why."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163440,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The history of the development of this telescope\nis a rocky one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167640,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The mission encountered many setbacks, from\ntechnological to the fiscal to the political."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173280,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Its cost grew to consume 25% of NASA’s entire\nbudget, before being axed by the US Congress,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179700,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then reinstated, and then streamlined, and\nfinally launched, just a decade or so after"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185640,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the original launch goal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186900,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The NGST became the James Webb Space Telescope\nafter NASA’s administrator for most the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192540,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "60's - a controversial choice all on its own\nthat I’ll leave you to Google."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198600,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After decades of planning and billions of\ndollars spent, everyone was relieved to say"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204480,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the least that the telescope deployment proceded\nperfectly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208380,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "JWST now sits at the Earth’s second Lagrange\npoint, its mirrors fully unfolded and its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215280,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infrared instruments humming along nicely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217440,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "JWST wasn’t planned just to be a bigger,\nbetter Hubble."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221760,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact it’s not that at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223380,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The telescope is focused on the infrared - that\nlight on the longer-wavelength side of visible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229560,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230280,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hubble, on the other hand, did visible light\ninto ultraviolet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234720,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The choice of long wavelength light specializes\nJWST for a number of particular science goals."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240600,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A big one is to study the early universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243420,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The very first galaxies shone with intense\nultraviolet light as the dense, young gas"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249180,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the early universe collapsed into the first\nstars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251580,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that UV light has now been traveling to\nus for most of cosmic history, and the expanding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256860,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe has stretched the light by more than\na factor of 10."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260460,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Energetic ultraviolet photons reach us as\ninfrared, stretched and worn out by the journey."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268080,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "JWST is designed to catch that light and has\nalready observed galaxies much closer to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273300,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Big Bang than ever before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274620,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "JWST is also great at exploring galaxies of\nany epoch of cosmic time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280020,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s IR sensitivity allows it to see the\ncool dust and gas that lives between the stars,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286500,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as well as peer through that dust which normally\nblocks shorter wavelength light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291060,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Combined with its sensitivity sensitified\nto the earliest galaxies this let’s us complete"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296340,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our map of galaxy evolution over cosmic history."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299040,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another key science area is planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301680,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "IR sensitivity lets us peer into the dusty\nwhirlpools around new stars and watch the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307260,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formation of planets in action."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309060,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It also let’s us see the atmospheres of\nplanets in other solar systems, which is an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313500,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "important step towards finding other life\nout there in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316860,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "JWST runs four separate instruments that allow\nit to take images as well as break light up"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322770,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into spectra, with sensitivities from visible\nred light through the slightly longer wavelengths"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327660,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of near-infrared all the way to the much longer\nwavelength mid-infrared."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332460,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It has various other tricks, like the ability\nto take spectra of many objects at once; or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337680,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "using a coronagraph to block the bright light\nof a star to better see its planets, to name"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342840,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just a couple."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344340,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so that’s a glimpse at what JWST can\ndo."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347460,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So now that humanity has this new toy, how\ndo we actually use it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352260,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start by looking at how most working\nastrophysicists hope to use JWST."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357480,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most of what the telescope will look at over\nits hopefully long life will be through the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362820,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "General Observer or GO program."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365700,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Through this program, anyone who has a good\nidea can ask nicely to borrow the multi-billion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371400,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dollar facility for a bit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373020,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And by ask nicely I mean develop a proposal\nthat lays out in excruciating detail the science"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379980,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "goals, the targets, the types of observation,\nand why of all the telescopes in or out of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385320,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the world only JWST has a hope of doing this\nand why these observations are going to advance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392400,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our understanding of the universe in really\nimportant ways and why YOU are definitely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397740,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to be able to pull off the project if\nyou get the data."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400500,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No big deal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401520,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you’re one of the lucky few to pass these\nhurdles, then you get a very happy email notifying"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407820,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you of your success."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408780,
    "dDurationMs": 8580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You celebrate for a few days, and get back\nto work on the next step - the phase 2 proposal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417360,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first step was the phase 1 proposal, by\nthe way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420060,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Phase 2 is when you lay out the exact observing\nplan in even more meticulous detail."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426060,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You give the precise locations on the sky "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428580,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of everything you want to \nlook at, what instruments"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431100,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will be used, the exact settings for those\ninstruments, the exposure times, etc. etc."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435600,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Do not mess this part up, because if you spend\nan hour looking at the wrong spot or with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441360,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wrong settings, you probably won’t be\nallowed to play with the telescope ever again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445500,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the final thing you need to do is wait\nfor your data."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449580,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "NASA queues up all the successful programs\nand executes them over the following year."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454260,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One day you’ll get another email with a\ndownload link to your data, at which point"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459180,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the real work can begin: analyzing the data\nand trying to bring to fruition that genius"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465660,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "idea you had, like, 18 months ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468480,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once you have your data, typically you have\na 12 month grace period in which only you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473760,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and your team get to access it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475560,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After that, the data becomes available to\nanyone in the world - I’ll come back to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480660,
    "dDurationMs": 180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480840,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These days, essentially all major observatories\nkeep modern, searchable databases of most"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486420,
    "dDurationMs": 840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of what they look at."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487260,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "JWST data is accessed through the Mikulski\nArchive for Space Telescopes, or MAST, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493980,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also includes the data from Hubble, Gaia,\nKepler, and many other space missions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499620,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The sheer quantity and richness of data in\nMAST and other archives is enough to support"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505200,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many astrophysicists without them ever having\nto write a single observing proposal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510240,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the reasons is that a lot of the data\ntaken by these telescopes - and certainly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514620,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "JWST - is actually meant for the entire scientific\ncommunity, not for individual researchers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520440,
    "dDurationMs": 7380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So-called legacy programs are all about maximizing\nthe value of data for as many scientists and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527820,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "science questions as possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529320,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A classic example is the Hubble Ultra Deep\nField, which is a million second exposure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534780,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that Hubble took of one apparently empty spot\non the sky, but which reveals 10,000 galaxies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541740,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back into the early universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543720,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many astronomers did a lot of science with\nthis one image."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547380,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "JWST will also do deep fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549960,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact it's already done one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551700,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the SMACS 0723 cluster."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554820,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "JWST pointed at it for 12 and a half hours\nacross different filters to produce the deepest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560580,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infrared image of any spot on the sky ever\ntaken."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564360,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For comparison, this is the Hubble image of\nthe same cluster."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568860,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those white blobby things are massive elliptical\ngalaxies of the cluster, several billion light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574920,
    "dDurationMs": 420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These arcs are much more distant galaxies\nwhose light is warped by the gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581400,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field of the cluster."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582420,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some of these things are shining at us from\nout of the very early universe - maybe when"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586920,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it was 7% its current age."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589560,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this bright star is, well, a star - in\nthe milky Way galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594120,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those pointy things are diffraction spikes,\ncaused by interactions of incoming light with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599880,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the hexagonal aperture of the scope."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601560,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there’ll be legacy programs to study\neverything else that JWST is good for."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607440,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These surveys often focus on areas of the\nsky that have been studied extensively by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612420,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other telescopes, which might seem redundant,\nbut JWST sees the universe very differently,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618360,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so studying the same objects in this new light\nreally deepens our knowledge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623700,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Oh, and a proper legacy program doesn’t\nhave a proprietary period, which means the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628920,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "data is available to anyone after it’s taken,\nincluding you if you want it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633780,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so that’s a primer on what JWST can\ndo and how astronomers use it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639240,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s wrap up by getting a bit more practical."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641940,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How could YOU get started doing something\nwith this telescope?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645480,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s actually pretty straightforward."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648540,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We don’t have time to do a full tutorial\nright now, so I’m going to link some resources"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652620,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "below."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653100,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But here’s an example from YouTube channel\ngalactic hunter, who shows you how to download"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "several images of the Carina nebula in different\ninfrared filters, and uses these to build"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664560,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the sort of beautiful color image that we\nusually get from NASA."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669060,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If anyone sends us their there own processing\nof JWST data we’ll try to show who it in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674700,
    "dDurationMs": 600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a future episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675300,
    "dDurationMs": 7380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve waited a long, long time for JWST,\nand its time to reap the rewards for our patience"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682680,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the hard work of many brilliant and dedicated\npeople."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686820,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I hope you enjoy the steady stream of amazing\nimages and even more amazing science that’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692940,
    "dDurationMs": 4596,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sure to flow in from Earth’s Lagrange 2\npoint over the next years as the James Webb"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697536,
    "dDurationMs": 7644,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space Telescope shows us things never before\nseen, and some never imagined, from the furthest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705180,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reaches of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707400,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’d like to thank Henson Shaving for supporting\nPBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710340,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Henson Shaving is the maker of the Al13 razor\nand even though I do have a beard, I do need"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716640,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a good shave from time to time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718620,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Created by a family run aerospace machine\nshop that’s built parts the Mars Rover and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724320,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the International Space Station, that expertise\nallows them to prevent the blade from flexing,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729960,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which results in a safe and smooth shave."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732420,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And by using standard double edged razor blades,\nyou’ll be able to use it without having"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737640,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to buy proprietary blades."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739200,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To learn more, just checkout \nhttps://hensonshaving.com/PBS"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743460,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re doing comments for the two recent\nepisodes - the one about the strong force"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748380,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and quantum chromodynamics, and then the one\nabout how the Higgs boson could tell us what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753120,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter really is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754140,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with the strong force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756660,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lassi Tiihonen and John Rizzo raise an excellent\npoint: our strong force video didn’t really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762540,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explain how atomic nuclei stick together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765060,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our explanation focused on how the strong\nforce causes quarks stick together to form"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770520,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hadrons like protons and neutrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772440,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We didn’t address how those protons and\nneutrons - those nucleons - then stick together"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778020,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to form multi-nucleon nuclei."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780720,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This probably deserves its own episode because\nthe process is pretty weird."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785100,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The force-carrying gluons don’t extend directly\nbetween nucleons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789780,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead, nucleons continuously spit out virtual\npi and rho mesons - quark, anti-quark pairs"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797040,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- which are then absorbed by neighboring nucleons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799860,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This constant exchange process keeps the nucleons\nbound together, analogously to how the atoms"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806700,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in molecules are bound by the exchange of\nelectrons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809580,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Robin Saunders reminds us that there’s a\nrelationship between gluon flux tubes and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815640,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "string theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817020,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That string theory actually began as an attempt\nto understand QCD, before being abandoned"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823320,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because it predicted a particle which behaves\nlike a graviton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826860,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And asks whether the methods of string theory\nare being brought back to understanding gluons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832140,
    "dDurationMs": 240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "again?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833400,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is yes, and thanks for bringing\nthat up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The core mechanics of string theory were developed\nto describe the very stringy one-dimensional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842400,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gluon bonds between quarks, particularly in\nmesons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846480,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They could be modeled as elastic threads with\nvarious vibrational modes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851940,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The model worked, but only because they look\na bit like elastic threads."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855840,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The approach was useful for prediction, but\nnot very explanatory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860520,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum chromodynamics actually started to\nexplain was going on, so it suplanted hadronic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867540,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "string theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868260,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as Robin notes, one of the vibrational\nmodes of these strings looked like the graviton,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874080,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so the entire theory was shrunk in size by\nabout 20 orders of magnitude and developed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880080,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into a theory of quantum gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881760,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We explain everything in our string theory\nepisodes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885420,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But now, decades later, with string theory\nstill failing to produce a slam-dunk testable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891420,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "prediciton, people are starting to take the\nprodigious math developed for the theory and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896880,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "apply it to other places - including back\nto where it all started - gluon behavior."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901500,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A couple of you also commented on the recent\nfinding that the proton seems to contain the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906900,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "influence of the charm quark, not just the\nup and down quarks as previously thought."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911880,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Indeed - and what a fascinating result."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914220,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That was published after we filmed this episode,\nso we’re going to have to do a new episode"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920040,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the finding."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921360,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stand by."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923220,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, on to our episode on how the Higgs boson\ncould be the key to discovering the dark matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929640,
    "dDurationMs": 660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931500,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mrwho995 points out that we forgot to mention\none of the important Higgs experiments - it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937800,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not just ATLAS at the Large Hadron Collider\nthat’s producing Higgs bosons, there’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942540,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also the Compact Muon Solenoid or CMS at the\nLHC."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947340,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it seems mrwho is part of that team."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950760,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the clearest signatures of a Higgs\nboson decay is into four muons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957180,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CMS is optimized for muon detection, so is\nalso a major workhorse for studying the Higgs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 963780,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s really hard to keep up with all the\namazing work that’s happening in physics,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969900,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so, mrwho995, thanks for helping us cover\nthe bases."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973560,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nyrdybyrd points out that the info from this\nHiggs episode is an influential argument for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980460,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a badass new particle collider."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983460,
    "dDurationMs": 840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a good point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984300,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ve heard it argued that it’s silly to\nbuild new particle colliders in the hope of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988440,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discovering particles that we don’t know\nexist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990600,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that’s not the only, or even best reasons\nto improve our particle collider capacities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995520,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, the discovery of the Higgs boson\nwas only the beginning - now that we know"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000020,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it exists, there’s so much we can learn\nfrom it if we can produce them more efficiently."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006140,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That said, there are various plans to build\nso-called Higgs factories that don’t involve"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011540,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just building bigger and bigger colliders."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014420,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m not saying we shouldn’t do that, but\nwe should also think about how to most cleverly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019040,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spend our science money."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021140,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Namishki901 feels as though digging smaller\nhas always returned more benefit than looking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028040,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bigger."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029840,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sure, quantum theorists of various colours\ncan tell themselves that they’re hunting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035120,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for the fundamental substrate of the universe,\nso far chasing their tails in the process."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041720,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Meanwhile we astrophysicists just get on with\nbusiness and discover the big bang, dark matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048080,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and dark energy, and figure out how the universe\nis going to end."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052280,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Did that sounds snarky?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1054740,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sorry."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1055700,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In reality we physicists are all just playing\naround with the small, special-case questions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061820,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about “physical” reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1063980,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just ask a mathematician."
    } ]
  } ]
}
